implementationPlan.description:
guest-session.mjs: endAgentJobs (DoD 3), plus test H8.

Add to scripts/local-runner/guest-session.mjs:
- imports: JOB_IDENTITIES from guest-bootstrap.mjs; invoker and QUERY_TIMEOUT_MS from lifecycle.mjs; `setTimeout as delay` from node:timers/promises. guest-bridge.mjs already imports lifecycle.mjs, so this adds no cycle.
- constants:
  - PKILL = Object.freeze(['sudo', '-n', 'pkill', '-KILL', '-U', JOB_IDENTITIES[0]]);
  - PGREP = Object.freeze(['sudo', '-n', 'pgrep', '-c', '-U', JOB_IDENTITIES[0]]).
  - Each runs as `['exec', '--no-map-working-directory', name, '--', ...argv]`.
- Why `pgrep -c`, a deliberate refinement of the task's argv. Exit 1 alone is ambiguous:
  - `sudo -n` exits 1 on its own failures (verified here: 'a password is required', empty stdout);
  - Multipass's own CommandLineError is 1 too.
  - `pgrep -c` prints the count, and with no match prints exactly '0' and exits 1 (verified: procps-ng 4.0.4, as in Ubuntu 24.04).
  - So verified requires exit 1 AND stdout matching /^0\r?\n$/. The CR allows for a Windows host's Multipass. That keeps DoD 3's "only on pgrep exit 1" and never claims termination it did not observe.
- `export async function endAgentJobs(name, provider = {}, { rounds = 3, sleep = delay, delayMs = 1_000 } = {})`:
  - Error handling: the invoker(name, provider) throw, rounds outside the safe integers 1..10, a non-function sleep, or delayMs outside the safe integers 0..60000 each reject with sessionError('SESSION_INPUT_INVALID') before any run call.
  - exec(argv) calls invoke([...], QUERY_TIMEOUT_MS) inside Promise.resolve().then(...), so a synchronous throw from an injected run is a transport failure too.
    - A resolved call is {code: 0, stdout}.
    - A rejection with an integer error.code and no error.killed is {code, stdout: error.stdout}, the remote exit (runGuestProbe's rule).
    - Anything else is a transport failure: a timeout (killed), ENOENT, or maxBuffer.
  - Each round:
    - sleep(delayMs) before every round but the first;
    - pkill. Its exit code proves nothing, so 0 and 1 are both fine, but a transport failure ends with verified false and runs no pgrep;
    - then pgrep. Exit 1 with '0' gives verified true. Exit 0 goes to the next round. Anything else (another code, exit 1 with other stdout, or a transport failure) ends with verified false at once.
  - Out of rounds gives verified false.
  - It always resolves Object.freeze({verified}).
  - No other user, process or provider command is ever run. Document that the provider is lifecycle's {executable, run}, so R10 final can pass one object carrying spawn and run.

Test H8 (timeout 30_000), with a scripted `run` that records (executable, args, options) and returns or throws per call. Rows give (results, verified, rounds), where rounds counts pkill/pgrep pairs:
- pgrep exit 1 '0\n': true after 1 round;
- pkill exit 1, then pgrep '0\r\n': true after 1;
- pgrep exit 0 '2\n', then exit 1 '0\n': true after 2, slept [7];
- exit 0 three times: false after 3, slept [7, 7];
- exit 1 with '' (the sudo case): false after 1;
- exit 1 '0\n\n': false;
- exit 2 '0\n': false;
- a killed timeout: false;
- pkill ENOENT: false, and no pgrep call.
Assert for every row:
- the result is frozen and deep-equals {verified};
- the args alternate exactly PKILL/PGREP full argv;
- every call is 'multipass' with shell false and timeout QUERY_TIMEOUT_MS;
- sleep got delayMs each time.
Invalid name, provider, rounds, delayMs or sleep each reject SESSION_INPUT_INVALID through rejectsWith.

Verification. Run it once, fresh, from the repo root:
`node --test scripts/tests/local-runner.test.mjs scripts/tests/local-runner-bundle.test.mjs scripts/tests/local-runner-bridge.test.mjs scripts/tests/local-runner-snapshot.test.mjs`
On WSL, run it as `env -i HOME="$HOME" PATH=$HOME/.nvm/versions/node/v24.21.0/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin node --test ...`, so the Windows PATH stays out. Exit 0 is required, and its output tail is the complete_task verification evidence.

Also confirm:
- `git diff --numstat` over the three files totals at most 400 changed lines. The architect prototype measured 201: bridge +17/-1, session +34/-3, tests +139/-7;
- no scripts/local-runner/__pycache__ exists;
- `pgrep -af 'time[.]sleep[(]600'` shows nothing of yours;
- LF line endings.
Peers' dirty paths are not yours: note them and continue.

Error handling for the gate itself: if a test fails, fix the cause with a test, never by weakening or skipping a row, then re-run the whole command.

Adversarial self-review of this task's diff only. Load the adversarial-self-review skill, then read the diff as each attacker in turn:
- A hostile guest: replying before reading the job frame; never reading stdin; echoing the credential in output; forging pgrep output through a Multipass or sudo failure.
- A careless caller: a job with role, a mutated job after the call, non-enumerable keys, prototype-named cli values, a credential with a newline.
- The environment: a Windows host's CRLF, a slow guest that keeps a zombie past 3 rounds, Multipass timeouts.

Check that:
- the job frame is the first stdin write on every path;
- the credential is in no argv, spawn option, env, error message, output or other provider call;
- the error handling never reports verified without exit 1 and a '0' count;
- the existing B1-B15, S, I, J and H1-H5 assertions are unchanged;
- verified-local, executionReady and every isolation claim are untouched;
- there is no .moe edit and no Git write.

Mutation spot-check in a scratch `git archive HEAD | tar -x` copy, never in the shared tree. Score each mutant by exit code with --test-name-pattern='^(B1[67]|H[1-8]) ' and wrap each run in timeout. Mutants and the test that should kill each:
- dropping `-c` or the stdout check: H8;
- treating exit 1 alone as verified: H8;
- no retry on exit 0: H8;
- retrying on a transport failure: H8;
- writing the frame after the first reply: H6;
- an unbounded first write: H6;
- role taken from job: H6;
- the count+hasOwn exactKeys: B16;
- a missing hasOwn on cli: B16;
- a value regex without anchors: B16.

Fix anything found with a test, then re-run step 4's command and supply that fresh output to complete_task.
